  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis at midday \nreveal shortwave ridging is in place across the High Plains, with a \nmodest shortwave trough upstream over the far southwestern CONUS. At \nthe surface, southwest KS resides on the southwest periphery of high \npressure, with a very weak pressure gradient resulting in nearly \ncalm winds across our area. These calm winds amidst a moist airmass \nhave supported a stratus deck/fog over our eastern zones through \nmuch of the morning that is only recently showing signs of erosion \nfrom west to east. Given the shortened solar insolation window, max \ntemperature grids within areas along and east of US-183 and near the \nI-70 corridor were lowered into the low/mid 50s while elsewhere will \nsee afternoon highs in the upper 50s/near 60. Overnight, surface \nhigh pressure and its attendant calm winds will likely foster \nanother low stratus deck and fog to develop roughly over the same \nareas as today, potentially requiring another dense fog advisory. \nOtherwise, lows will drop into the mid/upper 30s.\n\nDaytime Saturday, short range ensembles agree the aforementioned \nshortwave trough will dig further southeast into northern Mexico, \nbecoming largely split from the stronger westerly flow over the \nnorthern CONUS. With 500-mb heights rising over the central plains, \n850-mb temperatures will increase by 3-4 degrees C, which will \ntranslate to afternoon highs in the mid 60s.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles suggest \nlongwave ridging will dominate much of the central CONUS for the \nforeseeable future. Consequently, southwest KS will enjoy a \nprolonged period of above normal temperatures with highs in the 60s \nto low 70s along with anomalously weak winds. The flip side is no \nprecipitation is forecast as NBM pops are less than 5% during this \nentire period.",
